content.thinking:
Step 5: services. First IdempotencyService (with REQUIRES_NEW claim), then FareSplit logic, TripService, SettlementService. Also Clock bean config.

IdempotencyService design:

```java
@Service
public class IdempotencyService {
    private final IdempotencyRepository repo;
    private final PlatformTransactionManager txManager; // use TransactionTemplate for REQUIRES_NEW
    private final Clock clock;
    private final ObjectMapper objectMapper;

    public String execute(String requestId, String fingerprint, Supplier<Object> action) {
        // 1. check existing
        Optional<IdempotencyRow> existing = repo.find(requestId);
        if (existing.isPresent()) return replay(existing.get(), fingerprint);
        // 2. claim in new tx
        try {
            new TransactionTemplate(txManager).execute(propagation REQUIRES_NEW -> { repo.insertClaim(...); });
        } catch (DuplicateKeyException e) {
            // concurrent claim; wait for completion
            return waitAndReplay(requestId, fingerprint);
        }
        // 3. run business in a new REQUIRED tx (we're not in a tx here)
        try {
            Object result = txTemplate.execute(status -> action.get());
            String body = objectMapper.writeValueAsString(result);
            // mark completed in its own tx (or same as business? separate is fine)
            repo.markCompleted(...) in REQUIRES_NEW;
            return body;
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            // release claim
            delete in REQUIRES_NEW;
            throw e;
        }
    }
}
```

Note: business action itself is @Transactional service method? If action.get() calls a @Transactional method on another bean, and we're not in a transaction, that method starts its own tx. Good — so I don't need to wrap in txTemplate; the service methods (TripService.entry etc.) are @Transactional. But markCompleted should happen after business commits — it does, sequentially. If markCompleted fails, business is committed but claim stays IN_PROGRESS — replays would block. Rare; acceptable, but I can make markCompleted best-effort with its own tx.

Wait ordering issue: business tx commits, then markCompleted. Concurrent duplicate request waits for COMPLETED status — it polls. OK.

waitAndReplay: poll repo.find every 50ms up to ~10s; when COMPLETED → check fingerprint → return body or 409. If timeout → throw MetroException(409, "请求处理中，请稍后重试")? Better 409 CONFLICT status. Fine.

Replay check: fingerprint mismatch → IdempotencyConflict. If status IN_PROGRESS on first find → waitAndReplay too.

For claim: use TransactionTemplate with PROPAGATION_REQUIRES_NEW. Since IdempotencyService.execute is called from controller (no tx), plain repo calls auto-commit... Actually without a surrounding transaction, JdbcTemplate uses DataSourceUtils.getConnection — non-transactional, autocommit per statement. So insertClaim auto-commits immediately. That's exactly what I want for the claim. And business action runs in its own @Transactional. markCompleted autocommits. delete autocommits. So no TransactionTemplate needed at all, as long as execute() itself is NOT @Transactional. 

But careful: concurrent claim — second insert blocks on H2 row lock until first commits; autocommit means first commits immediately, so second gets DuplicateKeyException quickly. Then waits for completion. Good.

Business failure → delete claim → replay possible. But note: if business threw DuplicateTransaction (tx_no conflict), we delete the idempotency claim so the client gets 409 each retry — fine.

Clock bean: create `metro/config/MetroConfig.java` with `@Bean Clock clock() { return Clock.systemDefaultZone(); }`. Tests can override with @MockBean or TestConfiguration. Actually business times derive from request payload; clock only stamps created_at. Tests don't need to control it much. Still provide bean.

Fingerprint: SHA-256 of requestId + canonical string of business fields. Compute in controller from request DTO fields: e.g. `entry:txNo:ticketId:station:occurredAt`.

Now TripService:

```java
@Service
public class TripService {
    // entry
    @Transactional
    public TripResponse entry(EntryRequest req) {
        validate station exists
        transactionRepository.insert(...) catch DuplicateKeyException -> DuplicateTransaction
        // check no existing OPEN/PROVISIONAL trip for ticket? If ticket already has open trip -> 409 invalid
        long tripId = tripRepository.insertOpen(...)
        return response
    }

    @Transactional
    public TripResponse exit(ExitRequest req) {
        station exists
        transactionRepository.insert(...) // duplicate tx_no -> 409
        Trip trip = tripRepository.findOpenOrProvisionalForUpdate(ticketId).orElseThrow(TripNotFound)
        if (req.occurredAt.isBefore(trip.entryTime())) -> InvalidRequest
        int distance = mileageRepository.distanceKm(entry, exit) (null -> InvalidRequest unknown station pair)
        // chain
        long chainCumBefore = 0;
        Optional<Trip> prev = tripRepository.findLatestSettledBefore(ticketId, trip.entryTime());
        if (prev present && !prev.exitTime.plusMinutes(30).isBefore(trip.entryTime())) chainCumBefore = prev.chainCumKm();
        // note: prev must also have exit_time <= entryTime (query ensures) and window: entryTime - exitTime <= 30min inclusive
        long fare = fareService.chainedFareFen(chainCumBefore, distance);
        // overtime
        boolean overtime = Duration.between(entryTime, exitTime).compareTo(Duration.ofHours(4)) > 0;
        if (overtime) fare += maxNetworkFare;
        // batch date
        LocalDate natural = req.occurredAt.toLocalDate();
        LocalDate batchDate = natural;
        while (clearingRepository.findBatch(batchDate).isPresent() && sealed) batchDate = batchDate.plusDays(1);
        if (!batchDate.equals(natural)) insertDiff(natural, batchDate, txNo, trip.id, LATE_AFTER_SEAL)
        // reversal if provisional
        if (trip.status == PROVISIONAL) insertDetail(batchDate, trip.id, txNo, "PROV", 0, 1.0000, -provisionalFareFen, REVERSAL)
        // split
        List<LineMileage> lines = mileageRepository.lineMileages(entry, exit);
        split fare across lines -> insert SETTLE details
        tripRepository.markSettled(...)
        return response
    }
}
```

Wait — batch sealed check: findBatch returns only sealed batches (we only insert sealed). So `while (findBatch(date).isPresent()) date++`. 

Edge: chain lookup — prev settled trip exit_time <= this entry_time, and window: entryTime <= prev.exitTime + 30min. Also prev trip could be from a *previous* chain — chainCumKm carries. Good.

But subtle: prev settled trip might itself be settled AFTER this trip's entry (out-of-order registration)? findLatestSettledBefore filters exit_time <= entryTime — time-based, fine.

Split algorithm (in a helper, maybe `Splitter` class in fare package):

```java
public List<Allocation> split(long amountFen, List<LineMileage> lines) {
    int total = lines.stream().mapToInt(km).sum();
    if (total == 0) -> single allocation to first line ratio 1.0000 amount all
    long allocated = 0; list of amounts;
    for each line: ratio = round(km*10000/total)/10000 (BigDecimal, 4dp, HALF_UP)
